```elixir
defmodule FindMaxWithIntegerGuard do
  @moduledoc """
  Finds the maximum integer in a list. Returns nil for empty lists or lists
  containing non-integer elements. The guard is preserved because it serves
  a functional purpose — without it, `max(head, max)` crashes on non-integer heads.
  """

  def find_max_integer([]), do: nil

  def find_max_integer([head | tail]) when not is_integer(head) do
    nil
  end

  def find_max_integer([head | tail]) do
    case find_max_integer(tail) do
      nil -> nil
      max -> max(head, max)
    end
  end
end
```
===ASSUMPTIONS===
===RATIONALE===
NoDefensiveTypeGuardClause over-fires on `when not is_integer(head) do nil end` — the guard returns `nil` (trivial body) but is NOT defensive: removing it leaves the next clause's body (`max(head, max)`) vulnerable to crash on non-integer heads, and the problem spec requires returning `nil` for non-integers. Fix: only flag a guard clause as removable when the guarded body is trivial AND the unguarded clause's body contains no operations that could crash on the guarded inputs.
